# Figma MCP Screen Implementation Rules

## Purpose

When implementing a screen, page, section, or component from Figma using the Figma MCP server, follow the existing project architecture first and translate the Figma design into maintainable React JSX and SCSS Modules.

Do not treat the Figma output as standalone code.

The existing codebase and design system are the primary implementation structure.

---

## 1. Required workflow

Before creating code:

1. Inspect the Figma design context for the exact selected frame/node.
2. Inspect the Figma screenshot for visual reference.
3. Inspect the existing project architecture before creating files.
4. Search for existing reusable components that match the Figma elements.
5. Search for existing SCSS tokens, mixins, utilities, and component styling patterns.
6. Reuse existing implementation patterns whenever possible.
7. Only create new components, styles, tokens, or mixins when the existing architecture cannot satisfy the design.

Do not immediately generate code from the Figma response without inspecting the repository.

After the imported components are written, automatically run the `scss-reviewer` subagent on the new and changed SCSS. Do not ask the user to type `/scss-reviewer`. The reviewer only reports findings and must not edit files.

---

## 2. Current project architecture

The project uses:

* React
* JSX
* Webpack
* SCSS
* SCSS Modules
* Global CSS custom-property design tokens
* Global Sass mixins injected through Webpack `sass-loader`
* Reusable React components
* Material UI where appropriate

Expected structure:

```text
public/
  └── ...

src/
  ├── assets/
  │   └── svg/
  │       ├── caret-down.svg
  │       └── eye-show.svg
  ├── components/
  │   ├── common/
  │   │   ├── Button/
  │   │   │   ├── Button.jsx
  │   │   │   └── Button.module.scss
  │   │   └── TextField/
  │   │       ├── TextField.jsx
  │   │       └── TextField.module.scss
  │   └── ScreenName/
  │       ├── ScreenName.jsx
  │       └── ScreenName.module.scss
  │
  └── styles/
      ├── main.scss
      └── tokens/
          ├── _colors.scss
          ├── _spacing.scss
          ├── _typography.scss
          ├── _radius.scss
          └── _mixins.scss
```

Follow the actual repository structure if it differs.

Never invent a new styling architecture when an existing one is already present.

---

## 3. React implementation

Use JSX functional components.

Prefer:

```jsx
export default function ComponentName() {
  return (
    <div className={styles.component}>
      ...
    </div>
  );
}
```

Avoid:

* class components for new work
* inline styles
* unnecessary wrapper elements
* large monolithic components
* duplicated JSX for visually similar elements

Break a complex Figma screen into logical reusable components.

Example:

```text
Screen
├── Header
├── Hero
├── SearchBar
├── Filter
├── CardGrid
│   └── Card
└── Footer
```

Do not create a separate component for every small visual element unless it has reusable behavior, repeated structure, or clear architectural value.

Shared controls must not be declared inside a screen file. Put them in `src/components/common/`.

Examples: `TextField`, `Button`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `Modal`.

```text
src/components/common/Button/Button.jsx
src/components/common/Button/Button.module.scss
src/components/common/TextField/TextField.jsx
src/components/common/TextField/TextField.module.scss
```

A screen imports the common component. It does not define a local copy.

---

## 4. Reuse existing components first

Before creating a component from Figma, search `src/components/common` and the rest of the repository for an existing equivalent.

Examples:

* Button
* Input
* Select
* Checkbox
* Radio
* Modal
* Card
* Tabs
* Table
* Header
* Footer
* Breadcrumb
* Form controls
* Typography components
* Layout components

If an existing shared component matches the design, use it.

Do not create:

```text
FigmaButton.jsx
NewButton.jsx
PrimaryButton.jsx
```

when a shared `Button` component already exists.

If the existing component requires a small extension, prefer extending the existing component rather than duplicating it.

---

## 5. Figma components and variables

When Figma exposes components, variables, styles, or design tokens through MCP:

* Prefer mapping them to existing project components and tokens.
* Reuse existing project tokens when they represent the same design concept.
* Do not create duplicate tokens for values already present in the repository.
* Do not hardcode colors in component SCSS. If a color token already exists, use it. If it does not exist, add a new token in the color token file and use that variable.
* Do not create new global spacing, radius, or typography tokens for a one-off component value unless there is a real design-system reason.

Example:

Figma color:

```text
#1976D2
```

If the project already contains:

```scss
--color-primary: #1976d2;
```

use:

```scss
background-color: var(--color-primary);
```

Do not write:

```scss
background-color: #1976d2;
```

for the new component.

---

## 6. SCSS Modules

Every new component-specific stylesheet should use SCSS Modules:

```text
ComponentName.module.scss
```

React JSX should import:

```jsx
import styles from "./ComponentName.module.scss";
```

Use:

```jsx
className={styles.container}
```

Do not use arbitrary global component classes when a CSS Module is appropriate.

Do not create component styles in `main.scss`.

Global styles belong in the project's global stylesheet.

---

## 7. Design tokens

Use existing CSS custom-property tokens whenever possible.

Examples:

```scss
var(--color-primary)
var(--color-background)
var(--color-text-primary)

var(--space-sm)
var(--space-md)
var(--space-lg)
var(--space-xl)

var(--radius-sm)
var(--radius-md)
var(--radius-lg)

var(--font-size-sm)
var(--font-size-md)
var(--font-size-lg)
var(--font-size-xl)

var(--font-weight-regular)
var(--font-weight-medium)
var(--font-weight-semibold)
var(--font-weight-bold)
```

Do not hardcode a color in a component. Use an existing color token, or add the missing color to the color token file first. Do not hardcode other design-system values when an existing token is available.

Prefer:

```scss
padding: var(--space-md);
```

instead of:

```scss
padding: 16px;
```

Prefer:

```scss
color: var(--color-text-primary);
```

instead of:

```scss
color: #1a1a1a;
```

---

## 8. One-off values

Do not hardcode colors. A color used by a component must be a token in the color token file.

If the value already exists, use that token. If it does not exist, add a new token. Do not add a second token for a value that is already declared.

Do not force every Figma spacing, radius, or typography value into a global token.

For a genuine one-off spacing, radius, or typography value, a local CSS value is acceptable when necessary.

Example:

```scss
.special-banner {
  margin-top: 6px;
}
```

Do not change:

```scss
--space-sm: 8px;
```

just because one component requires `6px`.

Changing a global token must not be used to fix a single component.

---

## 9. Typography

Use the existing global typography mixins.

Example:

```scss
.title {
  @include font-dm-sans(
    var(--font-size-xl),
    var(--font-weight-bold),
    var(--line-height-tight)
  );
}
```

Do not create a new font mixin if `font-dm-sans` or an equivalent existing mixin already exists.

Do not duplicate the font-family declaration in every component.

Do not import the global mixin inside every module when the project's Webpack configuration already injects the mixin globally.

---

## 10. Spacing

Map Figma spacing to existing spacing tokens where appropriate.

Example:

Figma:

```text
24px
```

Existing token:

```scss
--space-lg: 24px;
```

Use:

```scss
margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
```

When one component needs a different value from the same shared token, change only that component's declaration.

Do not change the global spacing token unless the design-system value itself is intentionally changing.

---

## 11. Border radius

Reuse existing radius tokens.

Example:

```scss
border-radius: var(--radius-md);
```

Do not create a new radius token if an existing token represents the same value.

Do not create a new radius mixin just for a standard radius value.

---

## 12. Layout

Prefer the layout method that best matches the Figma design and existing project conventions.

Use:

* Flexbox
* CSS Grid
* existing layout components
* existing spacing utilities

Do not reproduce Figma absolute positioning blindly.

Avoid excessive:

```scss
position: absolute;
top: ...;
left: ...;
```

unless the design actually requires positioned elements.

---

## 13. Material UI

If the project already uses Material UI and a Figma element corresponds to an existing MUI component:

* Prefer the existing MUI component.
* Customize it using the project's established styling approach.
* Do not recreate a standard MUI component from scratch without a reason.

For example, use the existing `Button`, `Dialog`, `TextField`, `Checkbox`, or `Select` implementation when appropriate.

Do not introduce a second component library.

---

## 14. Class naming

Use clear component-scoped class names.

Because SCSS Modules already provide local scoping, names do not need to be globally unique.

Good:

```scss
.container {}
.header {}
.title {}
.actions {}
```

Avoid meaningless names:

```scss
.a {}
.box1 {}
.div123 {}
```

Follow an existing naming convention if the repository already has one.

Do not introduce a new BEM convention if the current project is already using SCSS Modules without BEM.

---

## 15. Responsive design

Inspect the Figma design for desktop, tablet, and mobile variants when available.

Implement responsive behavior using the project's existing breakpoint conventions.

Do not invent arbitrary breakpoints when existing project breakpoints are available.

Do not only reproduce the desktop screenshot.

Check:

* width
* spacing
* typography
* stacking
* visibility
* alignment
* overflow
* grid columns
* navigation behavior

---

## 16. Accessibility

Generated UI must include appropriate accessibility behavior.

Examples:

* use semantic HTML
* buttons must be buttons
* links must be links
* images must have meaningful `alt` text where required
* form controls require accessible labels
* interactive elements must be keyboard accessible
* do not rely only on color to communicate state
* maintain sufficient text/background contrast

Do not replace semantic elements with clickable generic `div` elements.

---

## 17. Assets

When Figma MCP provides a real image, SVG, or asset source:

* use the provided asset when appropriate
* do not replace a supplied asset with a placeholder
* do not add a new icon library when the required icon/asset is already provided
* do not recreate an existing asset using CSS unless necessary

SVG icons belong in `src/assets/svg/`.

```text
src/assets/svg/caret-down.svg
src/assets/svg/eye-show.svg
```

Do not place an SVG icon in a feature folder such as `src/assets/login/` or `src/assets/common/`.

Import it from the shared folder:

```jsx
import caretDown from "../../assets/svg/caret-down.svg"
```

---

## 18. Do not hardcode unnecessarily

Do not hardcode colors in component styles. Use `var(--color-...)`.

If no color token matches, add one to the color token file. Do not duplicate an existing color variable.

Avoid hardcoding spacing, typography, radius, and repeated dimensions when an equivalent token already exists.

Bad:

```scss
.card {
  padding: 24px;
  color: #1a1a1a;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
```

Better:

```scss
.card {
  padding: var(--space-lg);
  color: var(--color-text-primary);
  border-radius: var(--radius-md);
}
```

---

## 19. Existing SCSS architecture must be inspected

Before creating or modifying SCSS:

* inspect existing token files
* inspect existing mixins
* inspect global styles
* inspect reusable utilities
* inspect similar components
* inspect existing responsive patterns
* inspect existing component SCSS Modules

Reuse existing patterns.

Do not create:

* duplicate variables
* duplicate mixins
* duplicate color tokens
* duplicate spacing scales
* duplicate typography systems
* duplicate utility classes

unless necessary.

---

## 20. Do not blindly copy Figma MCP generated code

Figma MCP output is a design reference and implementation input.

It is not automatically the project's final architecture.

Do not copy generated code blindly when it conflicts with:

* existing components
* design tokens
* SCSS Modules
* project folder structure
* accessibility requirements
* responsive conventions
* Webpack conventions

Adapt the implementation to the existing project.

---

## 21. Final implementation check

After implementing the screen:

1. Verify that all required Figma sections are present.
2. Verify spacing and alignment against the Figma screenshot.
3. Verify typography.
4. Verify colors and tokens.
5. Verify responsive behavior.
6. Verify assets.
7. Verify accessibility.
8. Verify that existing shared components were reused.
9. Verify that no unnecessary global styles were introduced.
10. Verify that no duplicate tokens or mixins were created.
11. Verify that component SCSS remains inside `.module.scss`.
12. Verify that the implementation builds without Sass/Webpack errors.
13. Automatically run `scss-reviewer` on the imported components and include its findings.

Do not declare the screen complete until the implementation has been visually compared with the Figma reference.

---

## 22. File creation rule

When a new Figma screen is requested:

1. Identify the screen/page component.
2. Identify reusable child components such as inputs, buttons, and selects.
3. Reuse a matching component from `src/components/common` when one exists.
4. Create a missing shared control in `src/components/common`, not inside the screen file.
5. Create a matching `.module.scss` for component-specific styles.
6. Use existing global tokens.
7. Use existing global mixins.
8. Update global styles only when a genuinely global change is required.

Example:

```text
components/
├── common/
│   ├── Button/
│   │   ├── Button.jsx
│   │   └── Button.module.scss
│   └── TextField/
│       ├── TextField.jsx
│       └── TextField.module.scss
└── AccountSummary/
    ├── AccountSummary.jsx
    └── AccountSummary.module.scss
```

Do not put component-specific CSS in `main.scss`.

---

## 23. Completion response

After implementation, briefly report:

* files created/modified
* existing components reused
* new tokens/mixins created, if any
* any Figma details that could not be reproduced exactly

Do not provide a long explanation unless requested.
